/E (The State Design Pattern is implemented in the Order class to manage the different stages of an order in the food delivery system. Since an order changes its behavior depending on its current status \(such as Pending, Preparing, Out for Delivery, Delivered, or Cancelled\), the State pattern provides a structured and flexible way to handle these transitions.In this design, the Order class acts as the context class, containing a reference to the OrderState interface through the state attribute. Instead of using multiple if-else statements to check the order status, each state is represented by a separate class \(PendingState, PreparingState, OutForDeliveryState, DeliveredState, and CancelledState\). Each state class implements the updateStatus\(\) behavior differently based on the current order phase.The OrderState interface defines the common behavior for all order states, while concrete state classes manage how the order behaves and transitions between statuses. This improves the system by making it more organized, scalable, and easier to maintain, especially when new order statuses need to be added in the future.Overall, the State pattern increases flexibility, readability, and maintainability by separating status-specific logic into independent classes instead of keeping all logic inside the Order class)
/Pg 215 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
